; Text helpers — the part most worth testing on its own, which is the whole
; reason it is a separate file.
;
; AINL cannot index a string (`(nth "abc" 1)` is an error — `nth` takes a list),
; so everything here is `split` + list operations. That constrains what a
; tokenizer here can honestly claim, so it is stated rather than implied: words
; are whitespace-separated and `trim`med, which handles the punctuation that
; touches a word's ends but not an apostrophe in the middle.
;
; `trim` removes whitespace only at the *ends* of a string, so a newline in the
; middle of a line survives a space-split as part of a word. Replacing newlines
; with spaces first is what makes this work on a whole file; it is the kind of
; detail that is invisible until you read a text file, which is why it is
; written down here.

(def flatten (fn (text) (replace text "\n" " ")))

; Split on whitespace and drop the empties. `split` keeps *trailing* empty
; fields (docs/SYNTAX.md §3), so a file ending in a newline would otherwise
; contribute a final empty word; the `= w ""` check is what removes it.
;
; The accumulator is mutated by `def` directly in the `while` body, which does
; NOT open a scope (docs/SYNTAX.md §2a) — so `out` and `i` are the enclosing
; `let`'s own bindings and the mutations stick. Wrapping the body in a nested
; `(let ((w ...)) ...)` looks tidier and silently breaks this: a `let` body *is*
; a new scope, so `def out` inside it would create a binding that vanishes at
; the end of the iteration, leaving every function here returning `()`. That
; mistake is the reason the loop is written flat, and it is worth reading twice.
(def tokenize
  (fn (text)
    (let ((raw (split (trim (flatten text)) " "))
           (out (list))
           (i 0))
      (while (< i (len raw))
        (def w (trim (nth raw i)))
        (if (= w "")
          nil
          (def out (push out w)))
        (def i (+ i 1)))
      out)))

; The pipeline the caller wants: fold case, then split.
(def words (fn (text) (tokenize (downcase text))))
